{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC comment/description length convergence — and settle whether the comment limit is even right\n\n## Context\n\nCloud has two convergence suites that exist because oversized text caused real incidents:\n`mutate/test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` and\n`mutate/test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py`. Each drives an oversized value through\nTWO passes and asserts the second plans nothing — i.e. the truncated value the adapter sent must\ncompare equal to what it reads back, or the differ re-emits the change forever.\n\n**DC has neither.** And DC's comment path is the one with a question mark over it.\n\n## The specific doubt, verified in source\n\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:146-149`:\n\n    def sanitize_comment(self, body: str) -> str:\n        return _shared_sanitize_comment(\n            body, truncate=self._codec.fit_outbound, max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT\n        )\n\nTwo things are bound to the DESCRIPTION here: the limit (`WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`, 32767) and the\nfitter (`fit_outbound`, the description fit). Cloud does not do this — it routes comments through a\ndedicated `adapters/jira/comment_limits` module.\n\n**RESOLVED — and the original suspicion here was WRONG, so this section is corrected rather than\nleft standing.** This story was written believing that whether 32767 is the correct DC comment\nceiling was UNKNOWN, and that Cloud's separate `comment_limits` module existed \"precisely because\ncomment limits are not description limits\". Both are now settled against primary sources:\n\n* 32767 IS the correct DC comment ceiling for stock Jira >= 7.0 — it is the default of\n  `jira.text.field.character.limit`, whose own definition covers \"Description, Environment,\n  **Comments** and Text custom fields\". Comments and text custom fields are ONE property, so DC\n  binding both to one constant is faithful to DC, not a shortcut.\n* Cloud's `comment_limits` module exists because the UNITS differ, NOT the numbers: Cloud comments\n  are limited in PLAIN characters (32767) while Cloud descriptions are limited in ADF-SERIALIZED\n  size (`_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` = 32000, fitted empirically because `text_to_adf` inflates — a\n  32767-char description was measured serializing to ~50,566 ADF chars). DC has no ADF inflation,\n  which is why one constant genuinely suffices there.\n\nSo the substance of this story is NOT \"find out, then pin it\". It is: add the two DC convergence\nsuites DC lacks, and cite the ceiling's provenance at the binding so the constant stops reading as\nan assumption.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. THE CEILING IS ALREADY ESTABLISHED — do not re-measure it. Jira's own `jpm.xml` defines\n   `jira.text.field.character.limit` with `default-value 32767` and a description covering\n   \"Description, Environment, **Comments** and Text custom fields\", and JRASERVER-28519 records\n   that 7.0.0 made 32767 the default. Comments and text custom fields are ONE property by design,\n   so binding DC's comment limit to the same constant as its description limit is faithful to DC.\n   The task is therefore to CITE that provenance in the source at the binding, so the constant\n   reads as a decision rather than an assumption. Full evidence is in the research comment on this\n   ticket. (That the limit is admin-configurable, and so wrong on a reconfigured instance, is a\n   real but separate defect tracked as [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]; it does NOT gate this story.)\n2. Add the DC convergence suites as **SEPARATE FILES** in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/`.\n   DO NOT parametrize them alongside Cloud's. Cloud's two suites hardcode Cloud-only internals\n   that have no DC equivalent — `comment_limits.truncate_comment_body`\n   (`test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py:154`), and `ADF_PATH` /\n   `_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT = 32000` (`test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py:31-34`) —\n   whereas DC fits via `WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound`, a plain character truncation at\n   `WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`, with no ADF serialization step at all. Parametrizing would require\n   restructuring those incident-hardened Cloud tests, which this epic's own acceptance criteria\n   forbid (\"Cloud's pre-existing assertions still pass UNEDITED\"). This mirrors the split that\n   `cedc-58d1-f6d1-428e` names as its fallback; here the split is the DEFAULT, not the fallback,\n   because the blocker is already known rather than something to discover by trying.\n3. **INJECT THE DC BACKEND EXPLICITLY AT BOTH SEAMS. DO NOT DRIVE EITHER TEST THROUGH THE CONFIG\n   FALLBACK — it silently resolves to CLOUD.** This is the single most important instruction in\n   this plan and it was found by plan review, verified in source at 2ea5c3391d:\n   * `compute_outbound_mutations` (`outbound_differ.py:382-391`) accepts `outbound_mapper`,\n     `inbound_mapper` and `links` — but NO `sanitizer`. When any of the three is omitted it falls\n     back to `select_backend(load_config())` (`outbound_differ.py:431-441`).\n   * Worse for the COMMENT test: `outbound_differ.py:744-746` calls\n     `_diff_comments(ticket, jira_key, comment_snapshot, client=client, inbound_mapper=inbound_mapper)`\n     — it forwards NO `sanitizer`, so `_diff_comments` resolves one itself via\n     `_resolve_sanitizer(None)` -> `select_backend(load_config()).sanitizer`\n     (`outbound_comments.py:66-74, 205`), INDEPENDENTLY of any mapper the test injects.\n   So a DC comment-convergence test driven through `compute_outbound_mutations` would compare\n   bodies using CLOUD's sanitizer and converge against the WRONG truncation function — passing\n   while proving nothing about DC. That is exactly the vacuous-green this epic exists to eliminate.\n   THE REQUIRED SHAPES:\n   * COMMENT convergence: call `_diff_comments(...)` DIRECTLY with an explicit\n     `sanitizer=<the DC sanitizer>`. That keyword already exists (`outbound_comments.py:167`, the\n     ticket-21ca injection seam) — no production change is needed to reach DC's path.\n   * DESCRIPTION convergence: drive `compute_outbound_mutations` with ALL THREE of\n     `outbound_mapper=`, `inbound_mapper=` and `links=` bound to a DC backend. Injecting only\n     `outbound_mapper` is NOT enough: the guard at `outbound_differ.py:431` is an OR —\n     `if outbound_mapper is None or inbound_mapper is None or links is None:` — so leaving ANY of\n     the three unset fires the fallback block, which resolves the backend from config and can\n     overwrite the others with CLOUD's. Construct a minimal `JiraDataCenterBackend` over a stub\n     transport and pass `outbound_mapper=b.outbound, inbound_mapper=b.inbound, links=b`.\n   NO PRODUCTION CHANGE IS IN SCOPE HERE. If threading `sanitizer` through\n   `compute_outbound_mutations` looks desirable, that is a separate ticket, not this one.\n3. Only then decide whether `max_chars` needs changing. A limit change is a BEHAVIOUR change and,\n   if needed, belongs in its own bug ticket linked from here — not folded into a test story.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/` — NEW, DC-specific convergence files for comment and\n  description. Cloud's two existing convergence files must be left BYTE-UNCHANGED.\n- the `max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` binding in `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` — a\n  provenance COMMENT only, citing `jira.text.field.character.limit` and JRASERVER-28519.\n- NOT the limit change itself, and NOT making the limit configurable (that is\n  [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]).\n\n## Testing\n\nConvergence is a two-pass property, so a single-pass assertion cannot establish it: the test must\nrun the pass twice and assert the SECOND plans nothing for that ticket. A first-pass-only test\nwould pass against a bridge that re-sends the same truncated comment forever.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] an oversized DC comment converges: pass 2 plans no further change for that ticket, asserted\n      on the plan rather than on the absence of an exception\n- [ ] an oversized DC description converges, same assertion shape\n- [ ] the truncation suffix marker survives the round trip and is asserted, not just the length\n- [ ] the DESCRIPTION test proves it exercised DC's fit by a VALUE-BASED discriminator: DC fits\n      plain characters at `WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` (32767), while Cloud fits ADF-SERIALIZED size at\n      `_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` (32000). Those genuinely differ, so a length/ADF-shape assertion here\n      would FAIL if the fallback supplied Cloud's mapper\n- [ ] the COMMENT test proves it exercised DC's sanitizer by an IDENTITY or RECORDING check, NOT by\n      value. **A value-based discriminator is impossible here and must not be attempted**: DC's\n      `WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound` and Cloud's `comment_limits.truncate_comment_body` truncate at\n      the SAME limit (32767 / `_JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS`) and append a BYTE-IDENTICAL suffix\n      (`\" … [truncated by reconciler]\"` — `rich_text.py:69` and `comment_limits.py:46`), computing\n      `keep` the same way. Any assertion on the resulting string therefore passes under EITHER\n      sanitizer and is a test that cannot fail. Instead capture the injected DC sanitizer and assert\n      it is the one that ran — e.g. wrap it in a recorder and assert the recorder observed the call,\n      or assert identity against `dc_backend.sanitizer`\n- [ ] the comment test's mutation check targets the INJECTION, not the value: removing the explicit\n      `sanitizer=` must turn the cell RED via the identity/recording check. (A value assertion could\n      not detect that, which is precisely why the identity check is required.)\n- [ ] the comment test injects `sanitizer=` into `_diff_comments` explicitly, and the description\n      test injects ALL THREE of `outbound_mapper=`, `inbound_mapper=` and `links=`; neither relies\n      on `select_backend(load_config())` for any of them\n- [ ] mutation-checked for the injection itself: REMOVING the explicit injection (letting the\n      config fallback resolve the backend) turns the DC cells RED rather than leaving them green —\n      because a green-without-injection cell is testing Cloud while claiming to test DC\n- [ ] the `max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` binding carries a source comment citing\n      `jira.text.field.character.limit` and JRASERVER-28519, so a reader cannot mistake the 32767\n      for an assumption — verified by reading the comment in the diff\n- [ ] Cloud's two existing convergence files are BYTE-UNCHANGED (`git diff` shows no edit to\n      `test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` or\n      `test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py`), because the DC suites are separate files\n- [ ] the limit itself is NOT changed and is NOT made configurable by this story; that work stays\n      in [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]\n- [ ] mutation-checked: raising the limit above the real ceiling, or removing the fit, turns a\n      convergence test red"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785705166289501001,"uuid":"8f9c1dc9-037e-4d24-85df-409c1a92a0d6"}